 the computers that  you use  wow thank you  next question  thank you for your informative  presentation how long can the  autofluorescent proteins be preserved  ah this is a very excellent question  because it's of great practical  importance but juan has a good answer to  that  if you keep the sample  uh on pbs at least what we experience is  we can easily  keep a sample  uh in good conditions for up to a year  so if you have to of course change pvs  from time to time but if you keep the  sample in water then it's a problem  because the the  i mean fluorescence like the protein  starts to  uh degradate over time and that  then you can lose the the fluorescence  in one or two weeks  so what we do is usually if we need to  expand the sample we expand it for the  imaging session and after that you can  take the sample you can shrink it again  if you start to wash it with pbs  and then we keep it in pvs for longer  incubation  thank you  no at four degrees yeah you cannot froze  the samples because you have a polymer  gel that is filled with water then if  you put that in minus 20 it will  crystallize and everything is gone  all right  next question  how long  is the preparation suitable for imaging  does it fade out or degrade  uh it's it's i think that that's related  with the with the previous one so if  if you image with the sample in pvs  you don't have any problems so i at  least i have the experience to imagine  the same sample like like really long  just like for testing  but with water is is then you will see  the degradation over time  thank you  next question  would you tell us how the dendritic  spines and their heads and necks are  observed in your experiments  how they  again how they then treated the client  uh would you tell us how the dendritic  spines and their heads and necks  are observed in your experiments  oh i we didn't do any analysis uh on the  then on the spines like like the shape  of the um  yeah  i mean you can see that that slides so  we